 "author_id_missing": false,
 "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1635869/using-free-inside-the-destructor-of-an-object-freed-with-delete",
 "body_html": "<p>I have an object that I'm freeing with delete, and it has a <code>char*</code> that's being freed with free in its destructor. The reason I'm using free is because I used strdup and <code>malloc</code> in creating the <code>char</code> pointers. The reason I'm using <code>malloc</code> is because I used strdup to begin with in most code paths. Would this scenario cause memory corruption?</p>\n",
 "passes": [
  "main"
 ],
 "data_source": "so_main_cached (2026-10-05 collection)",
 "in_main_retained_480": true,
 "in_main_nonNA_coded_346": true,
 "in_offcwe_nonNA_coded": false,
 "events": [
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 1635884,
   "parent_id": 1635869,
   "author_user_id": 39742,
   "author_display_name": "Naveen",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/1635884",
   "score": 5,
   "created_utc": "2009-10-28T08:40:46Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2009-10-28T08:40:46Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>No, if you match calls properly i.e. <code>free()</code> for memory allocated with <code>malloc()</code> and <code>delete</code> for memory allocated with <code>new</code>, it will work fine.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 1635893,
   "parent_id": 1635869,
   "author_user_id": 19563,
   "author_display_name": "Lara Bailey",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/1635893",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2009-10-28T08:42:16Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2009-10-28T08:42:16Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>What you are doing is correct. A class that has been <code>new</code>ed should be deallocated with <code>delete</code>, but if it owns memory that was allocated with <code>malloc</code> (either directly or indirectly) then it should deallocate that memory with <code>free</code>.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 1635950,
   "parent_id": 1635869,
   "author_user_id": 57428,
   "author_display_name": "sharptooth",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/1635950",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2009-10-28T08:55:40Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2009-10-28T08:55:40Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>Your implementation is correct. You use free() to release memory allocated with malloc() (or strdup()) and this is exactly what to do.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement is that you release memory with a primitive matching the one used to allocate that memory. And this requirement is met in your implementation.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 1505111,
   "parent_id": 1635869,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 147192,
   "author_display_name": "Matthieu M.",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1635869/#comment1505111_1635869",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2009-10-28T09:02:35Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "I think that there is a misunderstanding here, your object only holds a pointer to <code>another</code> memory zone where the chars are actually stored. Therefore you are not freeing memory contained by the object, so everything is fine."
  },
